---
import BaseHead from "../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import { queryWordPress } from "../../lib/wordpress";
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from "../../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
    </head>
    <body>
        <Header />

        <main id="top">
            <section class="page-hero">
                <div class="container">
                    <div class="breadcrumb">
                        <a href="../index.html">Home</a>
                        <span>/</span>
                        <span style="color:#fff;">About</span>
                    </div>
                    <h1 class="fade-up">Roof Repair in <em
                            style="font-style:normal;color:#9CC0FF;"
                            >New Hampshire</em
                        >
                    </h1>
                    <p class="lede-page fade-up">Most roofs do not need replacing — they need one thing fixed properly. We find where the water is actually getting in, repair it so it holds, and tell you honestly if a repair is not the right call.</p>
                    <div class="hero-ctas">
                        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-blue"
                            >Get a Free Estimate</a
                        >
                        <a href="tel:+16035027080" class="btn btn-ghost-w"
                            >Call (603) 502-7080</a
                        >
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
         <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">What We Do</span>
      <h2>Repairs That Fix the Cause, Not the Symptom</h2>
      <p class="lead">Water rarely enters a roof where the stain appears on your ceiling. It travels along rafters and
        sheathing before it drops, sometimes several feet from the actual failure.</p>
      <p>That is why patching the visible spot so often fails. We trace the leak back to its source first — the lifted
        flashing, the split boot, the nail that backed out — and repair that. It takes longer than slapping sealant on a
        ceiling stain, and it is the reason our repairs hold.</p>
      <p>Vincenzo's Roofing repairs residential and commercial roofs across New Hampshire from our base in Seabrook.
        Every repair is quoted in writing before we start, and warrantied in writing when we finish.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ COMMON PROBLEMS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">What We Fix</span>
        <h2>Common Roof Problems We Repair</h2>
        <p class="lead">Most of what we get called out for falls into one of these. If yours is not listed, it is still
          worth a call — we have seen a lot of roofs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="probs">
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Roof leaks</h3>
            <p>Ceiling stains, damp attic insulation, or drips during heavy rain. We trace it back to the entry point
              rather than sealing where it shows.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Failed flashing</h3>
            <p>Chimneys, skylights, walls, and valleys. Flashing is where most roofs actually fail, and it is the most
              commonly botched repair.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Missing or blown-off shingles</h3>
            <p>Wind damage leaves the underlayment exposed. Left alone through one New Hampshire winter it becomes a
              much larger job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Cracked pipe boots</h3>
            <p>The rubber collar around plumbing vents dries and splits after about a decade. A cheap part, and a very
              common leak source.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">05</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Ice dam damage</h3>
            <p>Meltwater backing up under shingles lifts and loosens them. We repair the damage and address the
              ventilation causing it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">06</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Storm and impact damage</h3>
            <p>Fallen limbs, hail bruising, and wind-lifted sections — documented properly if you are filing an
              insurance claim.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">07</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Sagging or rotted decking</h3>
            <p>Soft spots underfoot mean water has reached the sheathing. We cut out the damaged section and rebuild it
              properly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prob"><span class="n">08</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Ridge vent and ventilation issues</h3>
            <p>Poor airflow cooks shingles from beneath and drives ice dams in winter. Often the real cause behind
              repeat problems.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ REPAIR VS REPLACE ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Honest Advice</span>
        <h2>Should You Repair or Replace?</h2>
        <p class="lead">We will tell you when a repair is throwing good money after bad — and equally, when your roof
          has years left and a salesman is overselling you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="col repair">
          <div class="head">A repair usually makes sense when…</div>
          <ul>
            <li>The roof is under fifteen years old</li>
            <li>Damage is isolated to one area</li>
            <li>There is a single, traceable leak</li>
            <li>Shingles are otherwise flat and intact</li>
            <li>The problem is flashing, not the field of the roof</li>
            <li>Storm damage hit one section only</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col replace">
          <div class="head">Replacement is the better spend when…</div>
          <ul>
            <li>The roof is past twenty years</li>
            <li>Leaks are appearing in several separate places</li>
            <li>Granules are filling the gutters</li>
            <li>Shingles are curling or cracking across the roof</li>
            <li>The deck is sagging or spongy underfoot</li>
            <li>You have already repaired it two or three times</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="text-align:center;margin-top:30px;color:var(--grey)">Still unsure? A <a href="/service/roof-inspection/"
          style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:500">free roof inspection</a> gives you a written answer — or read more
        about <a href="/service/roof-replacement/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:500">full roof replacement</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WHAT'S INCLUDED ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Scope of Work</span>
      <h2>What's Included in Every Repair</h2>
      <p>Whether the job is a single pipe boot or a rebuilt chimney flashing, the same standards apply.</p>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>Full roof inspection, not just the reported area</li>
        <li>Leak traced to its actual source before work starts</li>
        <li>Photo documentation of the problem and the fix</li>
        <li>Written quote before any work begins</li>
        <li>Matching materials wherever the existing roof allows</li>
        <li>Proper flashing and sealing, not surface patching</li>
        <li>Magnetic nail sweep and full debris removal</li>
        <li>Written warranty on the repaired area</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ COST ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cost-band">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color:#9CC0FF">Straight Pricing</span>
        <h2>How Much Does a Roof Repair Cost in New Hampshire?</h2>
        <div class="cost-figure">$350 – $2,500</div>
        <p>That covers the large majority of repairs we do. A few blown-off shingles or a cracked pipe boot sits near
          the bottom. A rebuilt chimney flashing, a repaired valley, or replacing water-damaged decking sits at the top.
          You get the number in writing before we start.</p>
        <ul class="cost-drivers">
          <li><b>What failed —</b> a pipe boot is a different job from a chimney flashing</li>
          <li><b>Access and pitch —</b> steep or hard-to-reach roofs take longer to work safely</li>
          <li><b>Hidden damage —</b> rotted decking found underneath adds material and labour</li>
          <li><b>Material matching —</b> discontinued shingles can be harder to source</li>
          <li><b>Extent —</b> one area versus several spots across the roof</li>
          <li><b>Urgency —</b> emergency and after-hours call-outs are priced separately</li>
        </ul>
        <div style="margin-top:30px"><a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-blue"
            style="background:#fff;color:var(--blue);box-shadow:none">Get a Written Repair Quote</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PROCESS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">How It Works</span>
        <h2>Your Roof Repair, Step by Step</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 01</span>
          <h3>Free inspection</h3>
          <p>We get on the roof, find the actual source, and photograph everything so you can see it for yourself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 02</span>
          <h3>Written quote</h3>
          <p>A clear price for the repair, plus an honest opinion on whether repair or replacement is the better spend.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 03</span>
          <h3>The repair</h3>
          <p>Most repairs are done in a single visit. We fix the cause, not the symptom, and clean the site before
            leaving.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">Step 04</span>
          <h3>Warranty &amp; proof</h3>
          <p>You get after photos and a written warranty on the repaired area. If that leak returns, so do we.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ NH WEATHER ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Local Expertise</span>
        <h2>Why New Hampshire Roofs Fail</h2>
        <p class="lead">The repairs we get called out for here are not the ones a roofer in a milder climate sees.
          Knowing the local failure patterns is half of finding the leak.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Ice dams at the eaves</h3>
          <p>Warm attic air melts snow mid-roof; it refreezes at the cold eave and backs water up under the shingles.
            The repair is only half the job — the ventilation causing it needs addressing too.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Salt corrosion on the Seacoast</h3>
          <p>Near the water, salt eats through fasteners, flashing, and drip edge years earlier than inland. We see
            failures in Seabrook and Hampton that simply do not happen in Concord.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Wind-lifted shingles</h3>
          <p>Exposed coastal and hilltop properties lose shingles to gusts, especially where the original nailing
            pattern was rushed. Repairs here need the correct fastener placement, not just replacement tabs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Freeze-thaw on flashing</h3>
          <p>Repeated expansion and contraction works sealant loose around chimneys and skylights. It is the most common
            leak we trace in older New Hampshire homes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ RECENT WORK ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Recent Repairs</span>
        <h2>Repairs Across New Hampshire</h2>
        <p class="lead">Every photo below is our own crew's work. Replace these placeholders with real project images
          before launch.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gal">
        <figure>
          <!-- IMAGE SLOT: <img src="/images/roof-repair-seabrook-nh.jpg" alt="Repaired chimney flashing on a home in Seabrook NH" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy"> -->
          <div class="ph">REPLACE WITH REAL PROJECT PHOTO</div>
          <figcaption><b>Seabrook, NH</b>Chimney flashing rebuilt after a persistent leak</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <!-- IMAGE SLOT: <img src="/images/roof-repair-hampton-nh.jpg" alt="Wind damaged shingles replaced on a Hampton NH home" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy"> -->
          <div class="ph">REPLACE WITH REAL PROJECT PHOTO</div>
          <figcaption><b>Hampton, NH</b>Wind-lifted shingle section replaced and resealed</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <!-- IMAGE SLOT: <img src="/images/roof-repair-exeter-nh.jpg" alt="Ice dam damage repaired at the eaves of a home in Exeter NH" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy"> -->
          <div class="ph">REPLACE WITH REAL PROJECT PHOTO</div>
          <figcaption><b>Exeter, NH</b>Ice dam damage repaired with ventilation corrected</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
        <h2>Roof Repair Questions</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item open">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">How much does a roof repair cost in New Hampshire?<span class="p"></span></button>
        </h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Most roof repairs in New Hampshire fall between $350 and $2,500. A handful of blown-off shingles or a
            single pipe boot sits at the low end. Rebuilding a chimney flashing, repairing a valley, or replacing
            water-damaged decking sits at the upper end. We quote the repair in writing before starting.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">How quickly can you come out?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Most non-urgent repairs are inspected within a few days and completed within the week. Seacoast towns near
            our Seabrook base are often same-week. Active leaks and storm damage jump the queue and are handled through
            our 24/7 emergency response with same-day tarping.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Should I repair my roof or replace it?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Isolated damage on a roof under fifteen years old is almost always worth repairing. Widespread granule
            loss, leaks in several separate places, or a roof past twenty years usually means replacement is the better
            spend. Our free inspection gives you that answer in writing, including when the answer is that your roof
            still has years left.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Will my insurance cover a roof repair?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Sudden damage from wind, hail, or a fallen limb is usually covered. Gradual wear, age, and deferred
            maintenance generally are not. We photograph everything, document the cause, and meet your adjuster on site
            so the claim is assessed on evidence rather than guesswork.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Can you repair a roof in winter?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Yes. We repair roofs year-round in New Hampshire. Cold weather changes how shingles seal, so some permanent
            repairs are staged: we make the roof watertight immediately, then complete the finish work once temperatures
            allow. We will explain clearly which applies to your repair.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3><button class="faq-q">Do you guarantee your roof repairs?<span class="p"></span></button></h3>
        <div class="faq-a">
          <p>Yes. Every repair is warrantied in writing. If the same leak returns in the area we repaired, we come back
            and make it right at no charge. That is also why we insist on finding the actual source of the water rather
            than patching where the stain appears.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SERVICE AREAS ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="margin-bottom:10px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Where We Work</span>
        <h2>Roof Repair Across New Hampshire</h2>
        <p class="lead">We are based in Seabrook, so Seacoast towns often see us the same week. From there our crews run
          statewide.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="tier-label">Seacoast — fastest response</div>
      <div class="chips">
        <!-- Switch each <span> to <a href="/location/seabrook-nh/"> once that city page is live -->
        <span class="chip pri">Seabrook</span><span class="chip pri">Hampton</span><span class="chip pri">Hampton
          Falls</span>
        <span class="chip pri">North Hampton</span><span class="chip pri">Rye</span><span class="chip pri">Exeter</span>
        <span class="chip pri">Stratham</span><span class="chip pri">Portsmouth</span><span
          class="chip pri">Dover</span><span class="chip pri">Rochester</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tier-label">Statewide</div>
      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip">Manchester</span><span class="chip">Nashua</span><span class="chip">Concord</span>
        <span class="chip">Derry</span><span class="chip">Londonderry</span><span class="chip">Salem</span>
        <span class="chip">Bedford</span><span class="chip">Merrimack</span><span class="chip">Hudson</span><span
          class="chip">Keene</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ RELATED ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:36px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Related Services</span>
        <h2>Other Roofing Services</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rel">
        <a href="/service/roof-replacement/">
          <h3>Roof Replacement</h3>
          <p>Full tear-off and rebuild when repairs stop being worth the money.</p><span class="go">View replacement
            →</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/service/roof-inspection/">
          <h3>Roof Inspection</h3>
          <p>Free, photo-documented condition report with a written answer.</p><span class="go">Book an inspection
            →</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/service/storm-emergency-response/">
          <h3>Storm &amp; Emergency</h3>
          <p>24/7 response with same-day tarping and insurance documentation.</p><span class="go">Get emergency help
            →</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/service/gutter-installation-repair/">
          <h3>Gutters</h3>
          <p>Seamless gutters sized to carry a New Hampshire spring melt.</p><span class="go">View gutters →</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FINAL CTA ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta">
        <h2>Get Your Roof Looked At — Free</h2>
        <p>We find the source, quote the repair in writing, and tell you honestly if a repair is not the right call.
          Most homeowners hear back within one business hour.</p>
        <div class="cta-btns">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-blue" style="background:#fff;color:var(--blue);box-shadow:none">Book a Free
            Inspection</a>
          <a href="tel:+16035027080" class="btn btn-ghost-w">Call (603) 502-7080</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
        </main>

        <Footer />
        <style>
            
            h1,
            h2,
            h3,
            h4 {
                font-family: "Montserrat", sans-serif;
                font-weight: 700;
                /* color: var(--ink); */
                letter-spacing: -0.02em;
                line-height: 1.2;
            }

            h2 {
                font-size: clamp(25px, 3.4vw, 34px);
                margin-bottom: 18px;
            }

            h3 {
                font-size: 19px;
                font-weight: 600;
                margin-bottom: 8px;
            }

            p {
                margin-bottom: 16px;
            }

            p:last-child {
                margin-bottom: 0;
            }

